Author: Ryan Arlett (ryanarlett9@berkeley.edu)
April-May 2025

Description:
This is the source code for a simple Win32 + DirectX11 based Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) renderer for rendering .ply files. It can also render meshes from .obj files.
I made this as part of a team project revolving around extracting meshes from 3DGS scenes.

Contents:
-Math.h: Vectors, Quaternions, and Matrices

-Globals.h: Shared variables and functions between Main.cpp and Graphics.cpp

-Resource.h/Resource.rc: VS resource file for the window's menu bar

-Main.cpp: The Win32 code for handling window creation, input, and file dialogs.

-Graphics.cpp: The DirectX11 code for both the GS and Mesh renderers. Also contains the code for parsing .PLY and .OBJ files, as well as the thread that asynchronously sorts the gaussians.

-GS_Shaders.hlsl: A vertex->geometry->pixel shader for splat rendering. Takes in gaussians, generates oriented quads for them, then determines their final alpha and color. I have annotated the math to make it helpful as a reference to others, as this took some effort to get right.
  
-Mesh_Shaders.hlsl: A very simple vertex->pixel shader for rendering meshes with diffuse lighting.

Acknowledgements:
All the GS rendering math comes from this 2001 paper: https://www.cs.umd.edu/~zwicker/publications/EWAVolumeSplatting-VIS01.pdf
This github (https://github.com/antimatter15/splat) was also incredibly helpful for seeing how to extract info from the ply files, as well as for the math in the GS shader. The idea to have a separate thread sort gaussians also comes from here. Unlike this github, however, I decided to have the gaussians themselves be the input to the vertex shader and to use a geometry shader to generate the quads, as that seemed like the more natural way to do it. 


